item.description:
arXiv:2608.23010v1 Announce Type: new Abstract: Tuza's conjecture states that for any graph $G$, the minimum size of a triangle transversal $	au(G)$ is at most twice the maximum size of a set of edge-disjoint triangles $
u(G)$. In this note, we prove $	au(G) \leq  rac{63}{22}
u(G)$, improving the previous bound $	au(G) \leq  rac{66}{23}
u(G)$ established by Haxell in 1999. The key observation is that for a "2-colorable" family of triangles $\mathcal{F}$, where each triangle has two blue edges and one red edge, we can obtain $	au(\mathcal{F})\leq (1+\sqrt{3})
u(\mathcal{F})$.
